{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# A characterization suite for the Data Center backend, mirroring Cloud's\n\n## Context\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py` (~373 LOC) is Cloud's byte-for-byte\nsafety net: it pins the priority map, the status map, sanitizer boundaries, identity label forms,\nprobe branches, and every branch of `map_fields_to_remote`. It hard-binds `_backend()` to\n`JiraBackend` at `:24-25`, so **none of it runs against Data Center**.\n\nDC's only unit-level backend coverage is `test_backend_contract.py`, which is parametrized over\nboth vendors but asserts presence and SHAPE only — \"the result is a dict\", \"the expected keys are\npresent\" (`:48`, `:60`, `:79`). It would pass with every VALUE wrong.\n\nThat asymmetry is exactly backwards relative to risk: Cloud's values were hardened by production\nincidents, DC's were written recently and have never been pinned.\n\n## Evidence\n\n* `test_backend_characterization.py:24-25` — `def _backend(): return JiraBackend(...)`. No DC path.\n* `jira_datacenter/backend.py:68-153` — `_DCOutbound`, `_DCInbound`, `_DCSanitizer` are separate\n  implementations from Cloud's, not thin wrappers.\n* `jira_datacenter/backend.py:46-66` — `_map_local_to_dc_fields` is a self-contained create-path\n  mapper, distinct from `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`'s.\n\n## The shared-module blind spot this also closes\n\n`jira_family/outbound_mapper.py` holds the SINGLE real `map_fields_to_remote` body (asserted by\n`test_rich_text_seam_heldout.py:168`), and it is currently exercised at\n`test_rich_text_seam_heldout.py:48,63,82` **only through `AdfCodec`**. Nothing drives that shared\nbody through `WikiTextCodec` at all, so a regression in the DC composition — a dropped length fit,\na lost non-`str` guard, a mis-mapped `status`/`priority` branch — would reach DC users with nothing\nto catch it. Driving the shared mapper through the DC codec is part of this story, not a separate\none.\n\n**What is NOT claimable here — stated explicitly so nobody writes a vacuous test.** The mapper's\nfit-then-normalize ORDERING is load-bearing (`outbound_mapper.py:59-79`), but it is **not pinnable\nthrough `WikiTextCodec`**: `WikiTextCodec.normalize_outbound` is the IDENTITY\n(`jira_family/rich_text.py:96-98` — \"Identity: wiki markup has no soft-wrap rejoin to normalize\"),\nso `normalize_outbound(fit_outbound(v)) == fit_outbound(v)` for EVERY `v`, and swapping the two\ncalls is unobservable on the DC path. An ordering assertion written through the DC codec would pass\nwhether the ordering is right or wrong — it would be a test that cannot fail, which is precisely\nwhat sibling story 2944 exists to delete. **Ordering therefore stays `AdfCodec`'s to pin**, where\n`normalize_outbound` genuinely transforms (soft-wrap rejoin) — which is why\n`test_rich_text_seam_heldout.py:76` can rely on `normalize_description(text) != text`. What the DC\npath CAN pin substantively is that the length fit is applied at all, and its exact boundary value.\n\n## Approach\n\nParametrize rather than copy. The Cloud file's `_backend()` seam is the natural fixture point:\nmake the vendor a parameter and let each case assert the values THAT vendor should produce. Where\nthe two legitimately differ (ADF vs wiki markup, accountId vs name), the parametrization must carry\nthe expected value per vendor rather than asserting sameness — this story must not accidentally\nassert that DC behaves like Cloud where it correctly does not.\n\n**Pin VALUES, not shapes.** The existing contract test already covers shape; repeating it adds\nnothing. Every assertion here should be one a wrong-but-well-formed value fails.\n\n**One documented EXCEPTION to the `_backend()` seam: the probe branches.** DC's `_backend()`\nfixture is `JiraDataCenterBackend(transport=FakeTransport())`, and that seam cannot see the real\nprobe classifier at all — see AC5 for the seam that must be used instead. Every other pin in this\nstory goes through the `_backend()` parametrization.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py` — parametrized over both backends,\n  or a sibling DC file if parametrizing turns out to obscure the Cloud pins (decide by trying the\n  parametrization first; a split is the fallback, not the default).\n- No production change expected. If a pin cannot be written because DC's behaviour is wrong, file a\n  bug and link it rather than adjusting the expectation to match the code.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe suite IS the deliverable, so its own credibility is the risk. Each new pin must be\nMUTATION-CHECKED: perturb the value it pins (one map entry, one boundary by one character) and\nconfirm that pin — and ideally only that pin — goes red.\n\nRecord each verdict TWICE, because the two records serve different readers:\n  * as a comment on THIS ticket (`cedc-58d1-f6d1-428e`), in the exact line format\n    `MUTATED: <symbol> <old_value> -> <wrong_value>; FAILED: <test_id>` — one line per pin; and\n  * as a module-level comment block in the test file itself, so the evidence lands in the diff and\n    stays reviewable in the repo after this session ends.\nA mutation is applied and reverted in a working tree and therefore leaves no artifact of its own;\nthese two records are what make it checkable.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the create-path mapper `_map_local_to_dc_fields` is pinned by VALUE for every key it emits —\n      `summary` (from local `title`), `description` (through `WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound`),\n      `issuetype`, `priority`, `status`, `assignee` — including each lookup's DEFAULT-fallback\n      branch (`\"Task\"` / `\"Medium\"` / `\"To Do\"`) and the empty-string fallbacks. It emits NO\n      project key — the project is threaded separately through `JiraDataCenterBackend.project` /\n      `JiraDataCenterTransport.project` — so nothing here pins one\n- [ ] both DC sanitizer boundaries are pinned at-limit AND one-over, including the truncation\n      suffix marker, for summary, description and comment\n- [ ] the shared `OutboundFieldMapper.map_fields_to_remote` is exercised through `WikiTextCodec`,\n      pinning that a `description` over `WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` comes back exactly equal to\n      `WikiTextCodec().fit_outbound(value)` — truncated to the limit and ending in the\n      `_WIKI_TRUNCATION_SUFFIX` marker — that a NON-`str` `description` passes through untouched by\n      the `isinstance` guard, and that `title`->`summary`, `status` and `priority` take their DC\n      values. This is deliberately NOT an ordering assertion; see \"What is NOT claimable here\"\n- [ ] DC's identity label form is pinned by value\n- [ ] DC's probe branches are pinned by value through a seam that reaches the REAL classifier.\n      `JiraDataCenterBackend(transport=FakeTransport())` must NOT be used for this:\n      `FakeTransport.probe_remote` (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py:140-144`)\n      hard-codes `ProbeResult(branch=ProbeBranch.UNREACHABLE, ...)` unconditionally and DC's\n      backend merely delegates to it (`jira_datacenter/backend.py:349-350`), so pins written there\n      would assert a stub's static return and could never observe the other three branches. The\n      real classification is `jira_family.classify_probe_response(..., resolved_statuses=...)`,\n      called from `JiraDataCenterTransport.probe_remote`\n      (`jira_datacenter/transport.py:438-457`). Pin it EITHER by calling\n      `jira_family.classify_probe_response` directly with DC's `DEFAULT_RESOLVED_STATUSES`\n      (`jira_datacenter/settings.py:37` — `frozenset({\"Resolved\", \"Done\", \"Cancelled\"})`; the\n      defaults are adequate for covering the four BRANCHES, but NOT for the cross-set discriminator\n      in the next criterion), OR by\n      constructing a `JiraDataCenterTransport` over a stub `jira.JIRA`-shaped client. All four\n      branches must be reached — `PRESENT_RESOLVED`, `PRESENT_FILTERED`, `ARCHIVED_OR_MOVED`,\n      `UNREACHABLE`\n- [ ] the probe pin also proves DC binds ITS OWN configured `resolved_statuses` rather than Cloud's\n      hardcoded set — and does so with a NON-DEFAULT set, because DC's `DEFAULT_RESOLVED_STATUSES`\n      (`jira_datacenter/settings.py:37`) is byte-for-byte identical to Cloud's\n      `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES` (`adapters/jira/probe.py:39`) — both\n      `frozenset({\"Resolved\", \"Done\", \"Cancelled\"})` — so at defaults the two are\n      INDISTINGUISHABLE and any such pin is vacuous. Pass a custom set (e.g.\n      `frozenset({\"Custom Resolved\"})`) and assert that a status in it classifies\n      `PRESENT_RESOLVED`, while that same status classified against Cloud's\n      `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES` comes back `PRESENT_FILTERED`\n- [ ] where Cloud and DC legitimately differ, the parametrization carries a DIFFERENT expected\n      value per vendor — no assertion claims parity where the adapters correctly diverge\n- [ ] [operator-attested] every new pin is mutation-checked, with the mutation and the resulting\n      verdict recorded BOTH as a comment on this ticket (`cedc-58d1-f6d1-428e`) in the format\n      `MUTATED: <symbol> <old_value> -> <wrong_value>; FAILED: <test_id>`, AND as a module-level\n      comment block in the test file so the evidence is a committed, diff-reviewable repo artifact.\n      Attested rather than CI-checkable because a mutation is applied and reverted in a working tree\n      and leaves no repo artifact to inspect\n- [ ] `test_backend_characterization.py`'s existing Cloud assertions still pass UNEDITED (a\n      parametrization that changes Cloud's expectations has broken its safety net)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785693380796923001,"uuid":"ae2e01f1-2237-4f39-aa8b-012f6929e379"}